E('middle ages|medieval|medieval period|dark ages', 'The Middle Ages is the period of European history between the fall of the Western Roman Empire and the Renaissance.', 'It is usually dated from about AD 500 to 1500.',
  'It is often split into the Early, High and Late Middle Ages.', 'Society was organised around feudalism, with lords, vassals and peasants bound by obligations.', 'The Catholic Church was the most powerful institution in Western Europe.', 'Castles, cathedrals, monasteries and universities all flourished.', 'The period saw the Crusades, the Black Death and the Hundred Years’ War.', 'Historians now avoid calling it the “Dark Ages”, since it saw real advances in learning, art and technology.',
  'Parliaments, universities and many European nations took shape in the Middle Ages.')
E('feudalism|feudal system', 'Feudalism was the system of land and loyalty that organised medieval European society.', 'It was most common from about the 9th to the 15th century.',
  'A king granted land, called a fief, to nobles in return for military service.', 'Those nobles granted land to knights, who swore loyalty in return.', 'Peasants, many of them serfs, worked the land and could not leave it without permission.', 'Everyone owed something to someone above them.', 'It declined as towns grew, money replaced service and the Black Death made labour scarce.',
  'Ideas about land ownership, nobility and contracts of loyalty grew out of it.')
E('charlemagne|charles the great', 'Charlemagne was king of the Franks and the first Holy Roman Emperor.', 'He lived from about 742 to 814 and was crowned emperor by the Pope on Christmas Day, 800.',
  'He united much of Western Europe, including modern France, Germany and northern Italy.', 'He encouraged learning, and his court produced the clear handwriting called Carolingian minuscule.', 'This “Carolingian Renaissance” preserved many classical texts.', 'Ironically, he struggled to write himself.', 'His empire was divided among his grandsons in 843.',
  'He is often called the “Father of Europe”.')
E('vikings|viking|norse', 'The Vikings were seafaring Norse people from Scandinavia.', 'The Viking Age lasted from about 793, with the raid on Lindisfarne, to around 1066.',
  'They raided, traded and settled across Europe, from Britain and Ireland to Russia and the Mediterranean.', 'Their longships were fast and shallow enough to sail up rivers.', 'They settled Iceland, Greenland and parts of Britain and Normandy.', 'Leif Erikson reached North America around the year 1000, centuries before Columbus.', 'Real Vikings did not wear horned helmets; that image comes from 19th-century opera.',
  'Days of the week like Wednesday and Thursday come from the Norse gods Odin and Thor.')
E('battle of hastings|norman conquest|william the conqueror|1066', 'The Battle of Hastings was fought between the Normans and the English and changed English history.', 'It took place on 14 October 1066.',
  'Duke William of Normandy claimed the English throne after Edward the Confessor died.', 'King Harold II had just defeated a Norwegian invasion at Stamford Bridge before marching south.', 'Harold was killed, traditionally by an arrow in the eye, and the English army broke.', 'William was crowned King of England on Christmas Day 1066.', 'The Bayeux Tapestry tells the story in embroidered pictures.', 'The Normans ordered the Domesday Book, a vast survey of England, in 1086.',
  'Norman rule brought French words into English and reshaped its nobility, law and architecture.')
E('magna carta', 'Magna Carta is a charter of rights agreed by King John of England.', 'It was sealed at Runnymede on 15 June 1215.',
  'Rebel barons forced John to accept limits on his power.', 'It said that free men could not be punished except by lawful judgement.', 'The Pope annulled it within months, but it was reissued several times.', 'Only a few of its clauses are still law in England today.',
  'It became a symbol of the idea that even rulers must obey the law, inspiring later constitutions.')
E('crusades|the crusades|crusade', 'The Crusades were a series of religious wars fought mainly between Christians and Muslims over the Holy Land.', 'The main Crusades took place between 1095 and 1291.',
  'Pope Urban II called the First Crusade in 1095.', 'The crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099.', 'The Muslim leader Saladin retook Jerusalem in 1187.', 'Richard the Lionheart led the Third Crusade but did not take Jerusalem.', 'The Fourth Crusade notoriously sacked Christian Constantinople in 1204.', 'The last crusader stronghold, Acre, fell in 1291.',
  'They increased trade and contact between Europe and the Middle East, and left a long legacy of mistrust.')
E('saladin', 'Saladin was a Muslim sultan of Egypt and Syria who retook Jerusalem from the crusaders.', 'He lived from 1137 to 1193.',
  'He was of Kurdish origin and founded the Ayyubid dynasty.', 'He defeated the crusaders at the Battle of Hattin in 1187.', 'He captured Jerusalem the same year, sparing most of its inhabitants.', 'He fought Richard the Lionheart in the Third Crusade, ending with a truce.', 'He was famed, even among his enemies, for chivalry and generosity.',
  'He is remembered across the Muslim world and beyond as a model of a just ruler.')
E('richard the lionheart|richard i', 'Richard I, known as the Lionheart, was King of England and a leader of the Third Crusade.', 'He lived from 1157 to 1199 and ruled from 1189.',
  'He spent only about six months of his reign in England.', 'He won battles at Acre and Arsuf but did not recapture Jerusalem.', 'On his way home he was captured and held for a huge ransom.', 'He died from a crossbow wound while besieging a castle in France.',
  'He became a legendary figure of chivalry, appearing in the Robin Hood stories.')
E('black death|bubonic plague|the plague', 'The Black Death was a devastating pandemic of bubonic plague.', 'It swept through Europe, Asia and North Africa from 1346 to 1353.',
  'It was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis, spread by fleas on rats and from person to person.', 'It killed an estimated 30 to 60 per cent of Europe’s population.', 'Symptoms included swollen lymph nodes called buboes, fever and blackened skin.', 'With so many dead, surviving workers could demand better wages.', 'Some communities blamed and attacked Jewish people.', 'Plague kept returning to Europe for centuries.',
  'It was one of the deadliest events in human history and reshaped European society.')
E('hundred years war|hundred years’ war', 'The Hundred Years’ War was a series of conflicts between England and France.', 'It lasted from 1337 to 1453, which is actually 116 years.',
  'It began over English kings’ claims to the French throne.', 'English longbowmen won famous victories at Crécy, Poitiers and Agincourt.', 'Joan of Arc helped turn the tide for France in 1429.', 'France eventually drove the English out of almost all its territory.',
  'It helped build national identities in both England and France.')
E('joan of arc', 'Joan of Arc was a French peasant girl who led French armies during the Hundred Years’ War.', 'She lived from about 1412 to 1431.',
  'She said she heard the voices of saints telling her to help the French heir, Charles.', 'At 17 she helped lift the English siege of Orléans in 1429.', 'Charles was crowned king at Reims soon after.', 'She was captured, handed to the English and tried for heresy.', 'She was burned at the stake in Rouen at 19.', 'She was declared innocent in 1456 and made a saint in 1920.',
  'She is a national heroine of France and a symbol of courage.')
E('agincourt|battle of agincourt', 'The Battle of Agincourt was a famous English victory in the Hundred Years’ War.', 'It was fought on 25 October 1415 in northern France.',
  'Henry V’s small, exhausted army faced a much larger French force.', 'English longbowmen shot down French knights struggling through mud.', 'Many French nobles were killed or captured.',
  'Shakespeare made it legendary in Henry V, with the “band of brothers” speech.')
E('genghis khan|chinggis khan|mongol empire|mongols', 'Genghis Khan founded the Mongol Empire, the largest connected land empire in history.', 'He lived from about 1162 to 1227 and was proclaimed Great Khan in 1206.',
  'His birth name was Temüjin.', 'He united the feuding tribes of the Mongolian steppe.', 'His armies of mounted archers conquered northern China, Central Asia and Persia.', 'He promoted soldiers on merit and protected trade along the Silk Road.', 'His conquests were extremely violent, and millions died.', 'After him the empire reached from Korea to Eastern Europe.',
  'The Mongols connected East and West, and Genghis remains a national hero in Mongolia.')
E('kublai khan', 'Kublai Khan was a grandson of Genghis Khan and the founder of the Yuan dynasty in China.', 'He lived from 1215 to 1294.',
  'He completed the Mongol conquest of China in 1279.', 'He made Khanbaliq, modern Beijing, his capital.', 'Marco Polo described his court in glowing terms.', 'His invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281 failed, partly due to storms the Japanese called kamikaze, “divine winds”.',
  'He was the first non-Han emperor to rule all of China.')
E('marco polo', 'Marco Polo was a Venetian merchant who travelled to China and wrote about it.', 'He lived from 1254 to 1324.',
  'He set out with his father and uncle in 1271.', 'He spent about seventeen years in China, serving Kublai Khan.', 'His book, The Travels of Marco Polo, was dictated in a prison in Genoa.', 'It amazed Europeans with descriptions of paper money, coal and huge cities.', 'Some historians question parts of his account.',
  'His book inspired later explorers, including Christopher Columbus.')
E('islamic golden age|golden age of islam|house of wisdom', 'The Islamic Golden Age was a period of great scientific and cultural flourishing in the Muslim world.', 'It is usually dated from the 8th to the 13th century.',
  'Baghdad’s House of Wisdom gathered scholars to translate and build on Greek, Persian and Indian works.', 'Al-Khwarizmi helped found algebra; the word algorithm comes from his name.', 'Ibn al-Haytham made breakthroughs in optics and the scientific method.', 'Ibn Sina wrote The Canon of Medicine, used in Europe for centuries.', 'Hindu-Arabic numerals, including zero, spread to Europe through this world.', 'The Mongol sack of Baghdad in 1258 is often seen as its end.',
  'It preserved and advanced knowledge that later fuelled Europe’s Renaissance.')
E('muhammad|prophet muhammad|islam', 'Muhammad is regarded by Muslims as the final prophet of God and is the founder of Islam.', 'He was born in Mecca around AD 570 and died in Medina in 632.',
  'Muslims believe he received revelations from God, which were collected as the Quran.', 'In 622 he and his followers moved from Mecca to Medina, the Hijra, which begins the Islamic calendar.', 'He returned to Mecca in 630.', 'By his death much of Arabia had united under Islam.', 'Islam spread rapidly afterwards across the Middle East, North Africa and beyond.',
  'Islam today is the world’s second-largest religion, with around two billion followers.')
E('ottoman empire|ottomans|ottoman', 'The Ottoman Empire was a powerful Turkish state that ruled much of south-east Europe, the Middle East and North Africa.', 'It lasted from about 1299 to 1922.',
  'It was founded by Osman I in Anatolia.', 'Sultan Mehmed II conquered Constantinople in 1453, renaming it Istanbul over time.', 'It reached its peak under Suleiman the Magnificent in the 16th century.', 'It besieged Vienna in 1529 and 1683.', 'It slowly declined and was called “the sick man of Europe”.', 'It fought on the losing side in the First World War and was dissolved; modern Turkey was founded in 1923.',
  'Its legacy shapes the politics, food and cultures of many countries today.')
E('fall of constantinople|mehmed ii|mehmed the conqueror', 'The Fall of Constantinople was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman Turks.', 'It happened on 29 May 1453.',
  'Sultan Mehmed II besieged the city for nearly two months.', 'He used enormous cannons to batter the ancient walls.', 'The last Byzantine emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting.', 'Hagia Sophia was converted into a mosque.',
  'It ended the Roman Empire for good and is often used to mark the end of the Middle Ages.')
E('aztecs|aztec empire|tenochtitlan', 'The Aztecs were a people who built a powerful empire in central Mexico.', 'Their empire flourished from the 14th century until Spain conquered it in 1521.',
  'Their capital, Tenochtitlan, was built on an island in Lake Texcoco, where Mexico City stands today.', 'It may have had over 200,000 people, larger than most European cities then.', 'They grew crops on floating gardens called chinampas.', 'Their religion included human sacrifice to their gods.', 'Hernán Cortés and his Indigenous allies conquered them, helped by smallpox.',
  'Mexico’s flag still shows the eagle on a cactus from the Aztec founding legend.')
E('maya|mayans|maya civilisation|mayan', 'The Maya were a Mesoamerican civilisation in what is now southern Mexico and Central America.', 'Their classic period was from about AD 250 to 900, though Maya people still live there today.',
  'They built cities with huge stepped pyramids, such as Tikal, Palenque and Chichén Itzá.', 'They developed the most complete writing system in the pre-Columbian Americas.', 'They were superb astronomers and created complex calendars.', 'They used the concept of zero.', 'Many southern cities were abandoned around the 9th century, possibly due to drought and war.',
  'Millions of Maya people today keep their languages and traditions alive.')
E('incas|inca empire|machu picchu|inca', 'The Inca Empire was the largest empire in the Americas before Europeans arrived.', 'It flourished from the early 15th century until the Spanish conquest in 1532.',
  'It stretched along the Andes, from Ecuador to Chile.', 'Its capital was Cusco, in Peru.', 'It had a road network of around 40,000 kilometres.', 'The Incas kept records with knotted strings called quipu.', 'Machu Picchu, a mountain estate, was built around 1450.', 'Francisco Pizarro captured the emperor Atahualpa in 1532.',
  'Quechua, the Inca language, is still spoken by millions.')
E('mali empire|mansa musa', 'The Mali Empire was a rich West African empire, and Mansa Musa was its most famous ruler.', 'Mansa Musa ruled from around 1312 to 1337.',
  'Mali controlled major gold and salt trade routes.', 'On his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, Mansa Musa spent so much gold in Cairo that its value dropped.', 'He is often called one of the richest people who ever lived.', 'He built mosques and made Timbuktu a centre of learning.',
  'He put West Africa on European maps, literally: he appears on the 1375 Catalan Atlas.')
E('samurai', 'The samurai were the warrior class of feudal Japan.', 'They rose in the 12th century and lasted until they were abolished in the 1870s.',
  'They served lords called daimyo.', 'Their code of conduct became known as bushido, “the way of the warrior”.', 'They fought with bows and, famously, curved swords called katana.', 'Japan was ruled by military leaders called shoguns from 1192 to 1868.', 'The Meiji Restoration ended samurai privileges.',
  'Samurai ideals of loyalty and discipline still shape Japanese culture and popular imagination.')
